    "answer": {
      "short": "$A=\\dfrac{pq}{2}$.\n\nPythagoras' theorem in the four right triangles gives $a^2+c^2=b^2+d^2$, so the squared difference in the supplied formula is zero.",
      "worked": "Let the diagonals meet at $O$. Label the vertices $B,C,D,E$ in order, with $BC=a$, $CD=b$, $DE=c$ and $EB=d$. Put $OB=x$, $OC=y$, $OD=z$ and $OE=w$. The perpendicular diagonals form four right triangles. By Pythagoras' theorem,\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2&=x^2+y^2\\\\b^2&=y^2+z^2\\\\c^2&=z^2+w^2\\\\d^2&=w^2+x^2.\\end{align*}$$\nThus\n$$\\begin{align*}a^2+c^2&=x^2+y^2+z^2+w^2\\\\a^2+c^2&=b^2+d^2\\\\a^2+c^2-b^2-d^2&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting into the supplied formula,\n$$\\begin{align*}A&=\\frac14\\sqrt{4p^2q^2-0^2}\\\\A&=\\frac14(2pq)\\\\A&=\\frac{pq}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nHere $p$ and $q$ are positive lengths.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
